First up is text. In programming, any piece of text is called a `string`.

To create a `string`, you must wrap your text in quotation marks (`""`). This tells the computer: **"Treat this as plain text."**

```javascript
// This is a string
console.log("Fatima Al-Fihri");

// This is also a string
console.log("مرحبا بالعالم");
```

**Output:**
```
Fatima Al-Fihri
مرحبا بالعالم
```

type: "quiz"
lang: "javascript"
question: |
  Which of the following lines of code will correctly print a `number`?
---
code: |
  // Option 0
  console.log("2024");

  // Option 1
  console.log(2024);

  // Option 2
  console.log('2024');

  // Option 3
  console.log(Year 2024);
---
solution: 1
options:
  - "Option 0"
  - "Option 1"
  - "Option 2"
  - "Option 3"
explanation: |
  Excellent! The value `2024` without quotes is a `number`. The values `"2024"` and `'2024'` are `string`s because they are wrapped in quotation marks.
